course: dictionary
lesson: 5883
title: Slang from Britain
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Slang is casual, everyday talk that people use with friends, and it may not be in every dictionary. Manky is slang from Britain and Ireland. When someone says «That's manky!», they mean it is gross: dirty, smelly or disgusting.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Manky socks» are socks that are dirty or smelly. A manky ball can be an old, worn-out ball, and food can be manky if it has gone off. The same word can describe dirt, poor quality or rotten food, so the rest of the sentence tells you which meaning to use.
explain: Manky is casual, not rude, so it is fine to say with friends. Do not use it in a formal letter or a school report. Check the words around it, swap in your meaning, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Manky is slang, used mostly in Britain and Ireland.
fact: «Manky» can mean dirty, smelly or disgusting, as in «manky socks».
fact: «Manky» can also mean of poor quality, broken or worn out.
fact: «Manky» can mean rotten or gone off, especially about food.
fact: Other British slang includes «grim», which means horrible, and «proper cracking up», which means laughing a lot.
exercise: «Sam kicked the manky old ball across the park.» What does manky mean here? | Light and bouncy | *Old and worn out | Shiny and brand new | Very tasty
exercise: «Mum said the milk was manky, so she poured it down the sink.» What does manky mean? | Creamy and sweet | Full to the top | Fresh and cold | *Gone off and rotten
exercise: «Ben's football socks were manky after the match.» What does manky mean? | Wet from the rain | *Dirty and smelly | Clean and folded | Very expensive
test: Priya says «That's manky!» about a muddy, dirty puddle. What does she mean? | Cold and wet | Lovely and clean | Very long and deep | *Dirty and disgusting
test: Which word is slang you would mainly use with friends rather than in a formal letter? | Ancient | Curt | Dirty | *Manky
exam: Which meaning fits «the manky old bike with a squeaky, broken seat»? | *Of poor quality, broken or worn out | Dirty and smelly | Brand new and shiny | Rotten, gone off, about food
